==English==

===Etymology===
From techniques traditionally used by stage magicians.

===Noun===
{{head|en|noun|head=[[smoke]] [[and]] [[mirror]]s}}

# A [[deceptive]], [[fraudulent]], or [[unconvincing]] [[explanation]] or [[description]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=March 21 1987|author=w:Ronald Reagan|journal=[[s:Presidential Radio Address - 21 March 1987|Presidential Radio Address]]
|passage=Budget process? It's more like a magic show. It's wink and blink and '''smoke and mirrors''' and pulling rabbits out of hats, but almost all that ever comes up are designs to hide increases for the special interests.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author=w:Australian Senate|title=Parliamentary debates Australia: Senate
|passage=On revenue raising issues, Mr Harris reveals what a master of '''smoke and mirrors''' Bob Carr really is.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|date=October 17 2009|author=w:Barack Obama|title=[[s:Barack Obama Weekly Address - 17 October 2009|Weekly Address: Taking the Insurance Companies on Down the Stretch]]
|passage=It’s '''smoke and mirrors'''. It’s bogus. And it’s all too familiar. Every time we get close to passing reform, the insurance companies produce these phony studies as a prescription and say, "Take one of these, and call us in a decade."}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 January 12|author=Nigel Harris|title=Comment: Unhappy start to 2022|journal=RAIL|issue=948|page=3|text=As for the IRP, Secretary of State Grant Shapps continues to peddle snake oil, '''smoke and mirrors'''. His reaction to near-universal IRP condemnation from politicians, local and national media, and all but a few rail specialists was to dismiss the lot of us (in the condescending and patronising tone we have now come to expect) as "critics and naysayers".}}

